We've covered _what you eat_ (Pillar #1) and _how stress rewires your gut-brain connection_ (Pillar #2). Today: the pillar that can be the hardest to change.

**Pillar #3: Lifestyle.**

Here's the thing nobody tells you: your gut bacteria don't just care about what's on your plate. They're also watching the clock and paying attention to your behaviors. The rhythms of your daily life affect gut health much more than many people realize - or want to admit.

Your microbiome runs on rhythm. It expects light in the morning, movement during the day, food at consistent times, and darkness at night. Break that rhythm (stay up late, skip sunlight, sit all day) and your gut bacteria get confused. Diversity drops. Resilience drops.

**_And here’s the catch: over the past 15 years of working with hundreds and hundreds of clients, I can easily say lifestyle is the hardest to change._**

No spoonfuls of sauerkraut, no supplement and no prescription can take the place of lifestyle habits that are messing with your gut (and your hormones, and your thyroid, and your metabolism… but I digress).

**The good news:** rebuilding that rhythm doesn't require a complete overhaul. Here are my top five small, consistent habits that I suggest you work on:

✔ **Sleep 7–9 hours, same bedtime.** Your gut bacteria have their own daily rhythm, and inconsistent sleep throws it off as much as a bad diet does. 10pm is an ideal bedtime for most people, and waking at the same time each day matters too.

✔ **Get outside within an hour of waking.** Morning light sets your circadian clock, and your gut's clock follows your body's lead. Drinking tea, eating breakfast outside, taking a short walk if possible - these are great ways to start the day.

✔ **Move for 20 minutes, every day.** Not a daily workout. Just movement. Walking, stretching, rebounding, yoga, dancing….Your gut lining and your mood both respond to it.

✔ **Get your hands in some dirt.** A walk in the woods, time gardening, even sitting in a park: you're picking up beneficial microbes just by being outside. Over-sterilization is actually killing your gut diversity.

✔ **Eating in a calm state - while sitting down.** A calm nervous system supports proper digestion. Eating while using your phone (or TV) distracts your brain from the digestive process, interrupting the signals that help your body properly digest and absorb nutrients Take time to relax between bites, slow down, and please (!!) don’t eat standing up.

**This week's challenge:**

Don't try to do all five. Pick _one_. Do it daily. Consistency is key, so work at one slowly until it becomes habit. I promise this has a bigger influence on your gut that you realize!

